@egarim the bug was that it was trying to deserialize even when the response was not successful. I fixed the bug in the develop branch. I put a [unit test here](https://github.com/MelbourneDeveloper/RestClient.Net/blob/dab3900b1ea55bb725f26055aa20117c2dda2e91/RestClient.Net.UnitTests/UnitTests.cs#L133) to reproduce the problem and make sure it doesn't come back.

Note that the [signature for the serialization adapter has changed](https://github.com/MelbourneDeveloper/RestClient.Net/blob/dab3900b1ea55bb725f26055aa20117c2dda2e91/RestClient.Net.Samples.Uno/RestClient.Net.Samples.Uno.Shared/NewtonsoftSerializationAdapter.cs#L12).

This is a breaking change so I will release this in version 4.0. I still have another small refactor I want to make before I release this version. I will let you know when 4.0 is released.

@intech-paul that's a very loaded question. Firstly, it's not good practice to send SQL over HTTP. I'd recommend building a GraphQL endpoint and then querying that end point. However, this is how you would do a POST

```cs
var client = new Client(new NewtonsoftSerializationAdapter(), new Uri(baseUri, resourceLink));
client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("x-ms-date", utc_date);
client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("x-ms-version", "2015-08-06");
client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("authorization", authHeader);
client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("x-ms-max-item-count", "5");
var response = await client.PostAsync<string, string>("SELECT * FROM SOMETABLE");
```

I've removed the resource because it looks like you are passing that in the base url.

Use Fiddler to make sure the transfer looks correct to you.

