@vincentsels I had a good look at this today. The unfortunate problem is that the underlying HttpClient class doesn't have this functionality. So, in order to achieve it, I'd have to shuffle the headers on to the client, and then pull them off again. That's really nasty and would end up causing all kinds of unexpected behaviour when in a multi-threaded environment. 

This comes right back to the poor design of HttpClient which is why I'm building RestClient.Net in the first place.

This won't make it in to 3.0 but as soon as there's a good solution - like finding an alternative to HttpClient, I'll implement this.

@vincentsels I would like to do something along those lines but probably not exactly that. Basic authentication is not so basic. What if it requires a bearer token?

There are issues around headers in general that need to be addressed. You can see it in the difference between put and post. 

I'm open to suggestions at this point but not rushing in to anything.
